export const articles={"imperial-county-unemployment":{"published":true,"title":"Why is unemployment so high in Imperial County?","seoTitle":"Why is unemployment so high in Imperial County?","description":"Local workforce plans and a monthly BLS series explain Imperial County's unusually high unemployment, including its farm seasons, border economy and limited job diversity.","deck":"The December 2024 map records 18.4% unemployment. Seasonal farm work and a narrow local job base help explain the persistent gap, but December was not the year's worst month.","body":"Imperial County, California, had an unemployment rate of **18.4% in December 2024**. Its job market is unusually exposed to seasonal agricultural work, with fewer alternative industries to absorb workers between jobs. The county's position beside Mexicali also connects its employers and workers to an economy extending beyond the U.S. border.\n\nThat explanation comes from local economic and workforce reporting, rather than from the unemployment number alone. The [2025–2028 Southern Border Regional Plan](https://www.ivworkforce.com/assets/3b.-southern-border-regional-plan-%282025%29---final-version.pdf), produced for the San Diego and Imperial workforce region, identifies seasonality, geographic isolation and a narrow employment base as continuing Imperial County constraints.\n\nThe rate means about 18 unemployed people for every 100 people in the civilian labor force, not for every 100 residents. Children and people outside the labor force are excluded. People with jobs are counted as employed even if they would prefer more hours.\n\n## December was high, but summer was higher\n\nThe [BLS series carried by the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/data/CAIMPE5URN) shows a repeating calendar within the year. These local rates are **not seasonally adjusted**, so the effects of recurring hiring and layoffs remain visible.\n\n| Month | Imperial County unemployment rate |\n|---|---: |\n| January 2024 | 17.9% |\n| April 2024 | 15.6% |\n| August 2024 | 21.2% |\n| December 2024 | 18.4% |\n| August 2026, a separate later observation | 21.9% |\n\nA seasonal explanation therefore does not mean December happened to catch the county at its annual peak. Even the lowest monthly rate in 2024 was 15.6%. The seasonal swing sits on top of a persistently difficult labor market.\n\nThe public USA Maps snapshot puts Imperial first among **3,143 valid county rows in the 50 states and D.C.** for December 2024. That is the coverage of the retrieved map series, rather than a claim that every Census county equivalent had a matching BLS observation.\n\n## Crops generate valuable output without guaranteeing year-round jobs\n\nThe [California transportation department's 2023 county forecast](https://dot.ca.gov/-/media/dot-media/programs/transportation-planning/documents/new-state-planning/transportation-economics/socioeconomic-forecasts/2023/2023-pdf/imperial-2023-a11y.pdf) placed agriculture behind only the public sector and health care among local employers. It described an economy producing cattle, feed crops and vegetables, including lettuce, spinach and melons.\n\nThose products require work at particular stages of planting and harvesting. Workers cannot assume that one completed crop job will immediately become another permanent position. The newer workforce plan reports that seasonal volatility remains part of Imperial's employment pattern through 2024, alongside uneven job recovery and limited diversification.\n\nThe scale of production can obscure that distinction. The [USDA's 2022 agriculture census](https://www.nass.usda.gov/Publications/AgCensus/2022/Online_Resources/County_Profiles/California/cp06025.pdf) records 623,755 irrigated acres in Imperial County. Irrigated land and valuable crops show the importance of farming; neither is a count of continuous jobs available to local residents.\n\n## A border economy is larger than the county's resident labor force\n\nThe regional plan identifies agricultural logistics, binational supply chains and border goods movement as important activities. Government and health care are also substantial employers. Its discussion of lithium, solar power and training describes attempts to broaden employment, rather than evidence that those projects had already eliminated joblessness in December 2024.\n\nThe 2023 Caltrans forecast specifically linked Imperial's longstanding unemployment gap with its seasonal workforce, daily movement between California and Mexicali, and a smaller share of workers holding college degrees. That is documented local context, but not a measurement of how many percentage points each factor contributed in 2024.\n\nThere is a further boundary to keep straight. The [employment statistics follow residents](https://labormarketinfo.edd.ca.gov/data/labor-force-and-unemployment-about-the-data.html); a count of jobs at Imperial businesses follows workplaces. A person living in Mexicali and working north of the border does not automatically belong in Imperial County's resident labor force. Cross-border activity helps explain the economic setting, not a license to add every border commuter to the unemployment denominator.\n\nImperial's problem is consequently more durable than a bad month between harvests. Its seasonal calendar produces pronounced fluctuations, while the local workforce plan documents a shortage of varied, stable alternatives. That combination explains why the number can fall in spring and still remain exceptionally high.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/imperial-county-unemployment.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/imperial-county-unemployment-640.webp","coverAlt":"U.S. county unemployment map highlighting Imperial County at California's border with Mexico, labeled 18.4% for December 2024.","map":{"metric":"unemployment","place":"06025","second":null,"caption":"Imperial County · unemployment rate · December 2024 · 18.4% of the civilian labor force. Source: BLS LAUS, not seasonally adjusted."}},"pitkin-income-comparison":{"published":true,"title":"Why does Pitkin County rank above San Francisco for income?","seoTitle":"Why Pitkin County ranks above San Francisco for income","description":"Aspen’s county has a higher income-per-person estimate but lower typical worker earnings than San Francisco. Investment income helps explain why.","deck":"Pitkin County’s income per person is $105,496, above San Francisco’s $92,289. Yet its typical full-time worker earns less. Money from assets helps explain the apparent contradiction.","body":"Home to Aspen, Pitkin County sits above San Francisco in the Census Bureau’s income-per-person estimates: $105,496 against $92,289. But switch to the [Census Bureau’s median earnings for people who work full-time throughout the year](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B20018), and the order reverses. Pitkin’s figure is $76,860; San Francisco’s is $116,476.\n\nThe first comparison includes money residents receive from assets as well as work. In Pitkin, interest, dividends and net rental income account for about 22% of all household income, compared with about 6% in San Francisco. That is a concrete part of the explanation: a place can have an exceptionally high income average without offering exceptionally high pay to its typical worker.\n\nThe full-time earnings comparison uses an additional Census table; this measure is not currently an indicator on the USA Maps map.\n\nAll these Census figures come from the [2020–2024 American Community Survey](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B19301). They estimate annual amounts using responses collected over five years, with earlier dollar amounts adjusted to 2024 buying power. They are neither five years of income added together nor a survey of 2024 salaries alone.\n\n## Money from assets changes the comparison\n\nThe Census asks about wages, net self-employment income, interest, dividends, net rent, retirement income, Social Security and other cash income. Its [income definition](https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/tech_docs/subject_definitions/2024_ACSSubjectDefinitions.pdf) measures money before personal income taxes. Capital gains from selling assets and increases in an unsold home’s or stock portfolio’s value are excluded.\n\nA resident can therefore contribute substantial income to Pitkin’s average through investments, regardless of whether that person holds a local job. A visitor who owns a second home does not automatically become a resident counted in that average.\n\nThe survey’s [household income totals](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B19064) show how different the two counties’ income mixes are:\n\n| Source of household income | Pitkin County | San Francisco County |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Wages and salaries | 59.8% | 81.9% |\n| Net self-employment income | 8.1% | 4.6% |\n| Interest, dividends and net rental income | 21.6% | 6.4% |\n| Retirement income | 5.9% | 3.3% |\n| Social Security | 3.5% | 2.7% |\n| Supplemental Security Income | 0.1% | 0.4% |\n| Cash public assistance | 0.1% | 0.1% |\n| Other cash income | 1.0% | 0.7% |\n\nBoth columns cover the full income total. These are shares of household income dollars, rather than the percentage of households receiving each type. Rounding means the displayed rows may not add to exactly 100%.\n\nThe finding also fits earlier local reporting. An [Aspen Journalism investigation published in April 2024](https://www.aspentimes.com/news/aspen-journalism-pitkin-countys-per-capita-income-third-highest-in-the-u-s-highlights-growing-disparity-with-wages/) traced the separation between residents’ investment income and pay from local jobs, alongside workers commuting from surrounding counties. Its figures used a different federal income measure; the table above keeps our comparison within the same Census survey.\n\nWhere those workers live matters. Someone who serves customers in Aspen but lives in another county contributes to that other county’s resident income statistics. The Aspen economy and the population inside Pitkin’s boundary are different groups.\n\n## The average can rise far above the middle\n\nPer-capita income adds residents’ income and divides by the entire population, including children. Large incomes pull that average upward. To see the arithmetic, imagine ten people receiving $50,000 each. Add one person receiving $1 million, and the average rises to about $136,000, even though the original ten still receive $50,000. This is an illustration, not a description of eleven actual Pitkin residents.\n\nA median finds the middle instead. The [household income median](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B19013) is $102,645 in Pitkin and $140,970 in San Francisco. Roughly half the households in each county are below its figure and half above.\n\nThat reverses the original ordering too. It shows why the income-per-person table cannot settle where a typical household has more money. A household may contain several people and earners; its median is a separate measure, not another version of the per-person average.\n\n## What if children are removed from the calculation?\n\nPitkin’s survey population is 16,985, including 2,484 people under 18. Divide the same aggregate income only among its 14,501 adults, and the result is about **$123,600 per adult**. Do the equivalent calculation for San Francisco and the result is about **$106,900**.\n\nThis answers a useful “what if”: changing the population used in the division does not reverse this comparison. But these are recalculations that leave the income pot unchanged. A true adult-only income average would also remove income received by 15–17-year-olds, whose income is included in the Census total. The tables used here do not isolate that amount. The $123,600 is therefore an illustration of the denominator change, not a published estimate of what Pitkin’s adults earn.\n\n## Where familiar places appear in the ranking\n\nSorting the published per-capita estimates puts Pitkin first among 3,144 counties and county equivalents in the 50 states and Washington, D.C. Here are some recognizable places on that list:\n\n| Familiar place | County geography actually measured | Income per person | Rank |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Aspen | Pitkin County, Colorado | $105,496 | 1 |\n| Manhattan | New York County, New York | $95,980 | 2 |\n| San Francisco | San Francisco County, California | $92,289 | 4 |\n| Washington, D.C. | District of Columbia | $77,348 | 15 |\n| Atlanta area | Fulton County, Georgia | $63,269 | 47 |\n| Boston area | Suffolk County, Massachusetts | $58,636 | 82 |\n| Brooklyn | Kings County, New York | $47,575 | 287 |\n\nManhattan is only one of New York City’s five boroughs. Suffolk includes Boston and other communities; Fulton includes part of Atlanta and other communities. Those boundaries help explain why familiar city names do not produce a neat city ranking.\n\n## Why Pitkin’s first place is less certain than it looks\n\nThe Census surveys a sample of residents, so its estimate has some sampling uncertainty. The margin of error expresses how much room to allow around that estimate. Pitkin’s published $105,496 comes with a margin of **plus or minus $13,363**, giving a range of $92,133 to $118,859.\n\nThat is a 90% confidence interval: if the sampling process were repeated many times, about nine in ten intervals calculated this way would contain the population’s actual average. It does not mean that nine in ten residents receive an income in that range.\n\nSan Francisco’s margin is much smaller, at $1,250. Sample size and the variation in what people receive affect precision; a small county with widely differing incomes can be harder to summarize reliably. Pitkin has about 17,000 residents, against San Francisco’s roughly 830,000. Population size is context, rather than a formula for the published margin.\n\nUsing the Census comparison method, the data do not establish a clear Pitkin lead over San Francisco at the 90% confidence level. The table orders estimates; it cannot award an undisputed first place.\n\nThe more useful discovery survives that uncertainty. Pitkin’s high average sits alongside a much larger investment-income share, lower typical full-time earnings and a lower household median than San Francisco’s. Aspen’s county illustrates how money received by residents can tell a very different story from the pay attached to their work.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/pitkin-income-comparison.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/pitkin-income-comparison-640.webp","coverAlt":"Pitkin County, Colorado and San Francisco County, California · average income per person · ACS 2020–2024 · dollars per person.","map":{"metric":"per-capita-income","place":"08097","second":"06075","caption":"Pitkin County, Colorado and San Francisco County, California · average income per person · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · 2024 dollars per person."},"kind":"accepted-example"},"los-alamos-doctorates-and-income":{"published":true,"title":"Why does Los Alamos have so many PhDs and household income near $150,000?","seoTitle":"Los Alamos: many PhDs and household income near $150,000","description":"Los Alamos combines an unusually large doctorate share with a $147,139 household income median. Lab payroll and real scientific vacancies help explain why.","deck":"Nearly one in five residents aged 25 or older has a doctorate, and household income has a $147,139 median. A national laboratory helps explain how those two features came together.","body":"Los Alamos County, New Mexico, has fewer than 20,000 residents. Yet nearly one in five adults aged 25 or older has a doctorate, and the median household income is $147,139. What produces that combination in such a small place?\n\nThe central explanation is Los Alamos National Laboratory: a large scientific employer whose work attracts people with specialist training and pays substantial salaries. Its own financial report supplies a direct connection to local residents. In fiscal year 2024, the laboratory paid about **$794 million in salaries to employees living in Los Alamos County**.\n\n## A community formed around a scientific mission\n\nLos Alamos did not develop as an ordinary town that later happened to acquire a laboratory. The federal government established the laboratory in 1943 to design and build an atomic bomb as part of the [Manhattan Project](https://www.energy.gov/em/manhattan-project-department-energy-formation-1939-1977). Its scientific mission helped shape the community from the beginning.\n\nThe institution remains a large employer. Its [FY2024 economic-impact report](https://cdn.lanl.gov/files/economic-impact-25-st-v4_73055.pdf) lists 16,547 employees, excluding contractors, and a total laboratory budget of $5.24 billion. That budget supports salaries, equipment, facilities and work across the institution.\n\nFor scale, the lab’s workforce is about **85% as large as Los Alamos County’s entire population of 19,435**. More than 65% of the employees lived outside the county, so the comparison shows the employer’s size rather than the share of residents who work there.\n\nAmong residents aged 25 or older, the survey estimates 2,590 doctorate holders out of 14,040 people: **18.4%**, compared with **1.7% nationally**. The laboratory provides a clear institutional reason for this concentration of specialist qualifications. The county figures do not identify which individual graduates work there.\n\n## High earnings show up beyond the household headline\n\nThe $147,139 household income figure is a median: half of households fall above it and half below it. A household can combine several people’s incomes, including retirement or investment income, so that number is not the typical scientist’s salary.\n\n<!-- article-map:household-income -->\n\nThere is a useful second check. For residents who work full-time throughout the year, [median annual earnings](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B20018) are **$108,894**, compared with **$61,657 nationally**. That comparison concerns individual workers, and it too points to unusually high earnings.\n\nThe education and earnings figures come from the 2020–2024 American Community Survey. Its income figures estimate annual income using responses collected across five years and express the amounts in 2024 dollars. The lab’s fiscal-year accounts are a separate source showing the scale and destination of its salary payments.\n\n## What scientific jobs at the lab actually advertise\n\nThe laboratory’s own vacancies make the pay easier to picture. These examples were listed on its careers site when checked on **October 26, 2026**:\n\n| Scientific role | Advertised annual salary |\n|---|---:|\n| [Astrophysics postdoctoral researcher](https://lanl.jobs/search/jobdetails/astrophysics-postdoctoral-researcher/53d46566-456a-46a9-8107-755a725b2849) | From $97,000 |\n| [Computational electrodynamics accelerator physicist, Scientist 2](https://lanl.jobs/search/jobdetails/computational-electrodynamics-accelerator-physicist-23/213d645f-ecbe-4939-9bd6-34dd41f5b6ad) | $106,400–$176,000 |\n| [Senior nuclear diagnostics and imaging physicist, Scientist 4](https://lanl.jobs/search/jobdetails/senior-nuclear-diagnostics-and-imaging-physicist-scientist-34/d9835153-b74a-402c-a407-41af4ed870a1) | $153,600–$266,500 |\n\nThese are salary offers for identified positions and levels, rather than an average for all scientists. They show the pay attached to specific research work; they are 2026 examples alongside the earlier Census income figures.\n\nThe salary money also reaches neighboring communities. In FY2024, the lab paid roughly $504 million to employees living in Santa Fe County and $215 million to those in Rio Arriba County, as well as the $794 million paid to Los Alamos County residents.\n\nA scientific institution helped form Los Alamos in 1943 and still sends hundreds of millions of dollars in salaries to county residents. That enduring connection between research, specialist workers and paid work explains why advanced degrees and high incomes appear together on this small patch of New Mexico.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/los-alamos-doctorates-and-income.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/los-alamos-doctorates-and-income-640.webp","coverAlt":"Los Alamos County, New Mexico · adults aged 25+ with a doctorate · ACS 2020–2024 · percent.","map":{"metric":"doctorate","place":"35028","second":null,"caption":"Los Alamos County, New Mexico · adults aged 25+ with a doctorate · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · percent."},"kind":"accepted-example"},"nassau-bronx-household-income":{"published":true,"title":"Two miles of water, three times the income: why Nassau and the Bronx are worlds apart","seoTitle":"Why Nassau’s household income is three times the Bronx’s","description":"Across a short stretch of Long Island Sound, Nassau and the Bronx have very different household incomes. Earnings, multiple earners and housing help explain why.","deck":"Their shorelines face each other across Long Island Sound. But differences in earnings, family income and access to housing help explain why Nassau’s household median is $146,202 and the Bronx’s is $48,676.","body":"At the western end of Long Island Sound, roughly two miles of water separate Fort Schuyler in the Bronx from Kings Point in Nassau County. On a map, they are near neighbors. In the Census Bureau’s household-income figures, they look much further apart: $48,676 in the Bronx against $146,202 in Nassau.\n\nNassau’s household income is about three times as high. Its residents typically earn more from work, and more families combine income from two or more earners. Higher housing costs and restrictions on cheaper homes also help determine who can afford to live there.\n\nBoth headline figures are [median household incomes](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B19013), the midpoint at which half of households have more income and half have less. They combine cash income received by household members before personal income taxes. They are not individual salaries.\n\n## The gap remains when we compare individual workers\n\nA household’s income depends partly on what each earner receives. Among people who work full-time throughout the year, the [median annual earnings](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B20018) are **$89,403 for Nassau residents and $51,826 for Bronx residents**.\n\nThat is a smaller gap than the threefold household comparison, but still a large one. Comparing individual full-time workers therefore rules out the idea that the entire household gap is simply a difference in how many people share a home.\n\nThe kind of work residents do also differs. About 51% of Nassau’s employed civilian residents work in the Census category covering management, business, science and arts occupations; in the Bronx, about 29% do. The figures follow residents, including those who commute elsewhere, rather than counting only jobs located within their home county.\n\nEducation helps explain access to many of those professional roles. Nearly half of Nassau’s residents aged 25 or older have at least a bachelor’s degree, compared with about 22% in the Bronx. These are differences in the opportunities and qualifications represented in each resident population, rather than a measure of how hard people work.\n\n| The same Census survey, different resident profiles | Nassau County | Bronx County |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Median household income | $146,202 | $48,676 |\n| Median earnings, full-time year-round workers aged 16+ | $89,403 | $51,826 |\n| Adults aged 25+ with at least a bachelor’s degree | 49.7% | 22.4% |\n| Employed civilian residents aged 16+ in management, business, science and arts occupations | 51.2% | 29.3% |\n\nAll four comparisons use the 2020–2024 American Community Survey. It pools responses collected over five years; the money figures are annual amounts expressed in 2024 dollars.\n\n## More families in Nassau combine two or more earners\n\nThere is another way the same individual earnings can produce different household incomes: one family may have a single paycheck, another two or three.\n\nThe survey estimates that **63% of families in Nassau have at least two earners**, compared with **45% in the Bronx**. An earner here is someone who received wages or self-employment earnings during the preceding 12 months, including part-time work.\n\nThis comparison concerns families, a subset of households whose members are related. It does not include every person living alone or every group of unrelated housemates. It nevertheless identifies a relevant difference: a larger share of Nassau’s families can combine income from several workers.\n\nIndividual earnings and the number of earners both point in the same direction: Nassau’s families more often combine several earnings, while its typical full-time worker also earns more. The summary tables do not assign each factor an exact share of the $97,526 household gap.\n\n## Housing helps decide who lives on each side\n\nPeople do not enter the two county populations at random. The price and availability of homes affect which incomes a household needs to live in a particular community.\n\nThe same Census survey shows higher housing costs in Nassau, although the gap is much smaller than the threefold income difference:\n\n| Housing measure, ACS 2020–2024, 2024 dollars | Nassau County | Bronx County |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Median value of an owner-occupied home | $684,700 | $529,500 |\n| Median monthly gross rent | $2,252 | $1,458 |\n\nThe [home value](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25077) is the owner’s estimate of what the property would sell for. [Gross rent](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25064) includes rent and applicable tenant-paid utilities. These figures describe homes already occupied during the survey period, rather than today’s price for a newly available, renovated apartment.\n\nFor someone looking for a modern two-bedroom home now, the asking rent can be much higher. Here are two examples advertised when checked on **October 26, 2026**:\n\n| Modern apartment example | Bedrooms / bathrooms | Monthly base rent |\n|---|---|---:|\n| [Estela, Paragon 2, unit E1016](https://www.rentcafe.com/housing/ny/bronx/estela-properties/default.aspx), Mott Haven, Bronx | 2 / 2 | $4,007 |\n| [Morgan Parc, unit E410](https://www.apartments.com/morgan-parc-mineola-ny/5hjl7mm/), Mineola, Nassau County | 2 / 2 | $5,550 |\n\nBoth developments advertise contemporary interiors and in-unit laundry. They are examples from the modern apartment market, with different floor plans and sizes; they do not establish either county’s average asking rent. The links show the interiors and floor plans, and extra charges and included utilities vary.\n\nFor the Nassau example, $5,550 a month means **$66,600 a year in base rent alone**. If a household wanted that rent to take 30% of its income before tax, it would need **$222,000 a year**. That is a budget illustration, not a verified minimum-income rule for Morgan Parc. It shows why the older $2,252 county rent median is a poor guide to the cost of moving into this particular kind of home today.\n\nIn a [2023 study of Long Island housing and schools](https://tcf.org/content/report/housing-and-educational-inequality-the-case-of-long-island/), The Century Foundation and collaborating NYU Furman Center researchers examined exclusionary zoning, including restrictions on multifamily homes and minimum lot sizes. Such rules can limit the cheaper housing options through which lower-income families might enter affluent communities.\n\nThe study also documented large differences within Nassau itself, using Flower Hill and the Village of Hempstead as examples. Nassau’s high county median does not mean that all its neighborhoods are wealthy or share the same access to schools and housing.\n\nThat history is part of how nearby communities can develop very different resident populations. Housing costs and restrictions help concentrate higher incomes in some places, while access to schooling and professional work influences the incomes people can earn. A line on the map records the resulting pattern; it does not create an equal starting point on either side.\n\nCrossing the water would not triple the pay of a Bronx worker. Nassau’s threefold household figure describes a different mix of earnings, earners and residents, shaped in part by who can afford and obtain housing there. The short distance makes the contrast striking. The differences in work and access to a home make it understandable.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/nassau-bronx-household-income.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/nassau-bronx-household-income-640.webp","coverAlt":"Nassau County and Bronx County, New York · median household income · ACS 2020–2024 · 2024 dollars per household.","map":{"metric":"median-household-income","place":"36059","second":"36005","caption":"Nassau County and Bronx County, New York · median household income · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · 2024 dollars per household."}},"california-tax-return-income":{"published":true,"title":"Why California leads the tax-return income table at $2.05 trillion","seoTitle":"Why California leads the tax-return income table at $2.05 trillion","description":"See the five largest state AGI totals and their return counts. Original IRS county data and a documented California jobs cluster explain what drives the comparison.","deck":"California's 2023 county-file total combines 18.48 million returns with about $111,000 in adjusted gross income per return. The result is an income ranking, not a GDP table.","body":"California leads this tax-return income comparison with **$2.051 trillion**, followed by Texas, Florida and New York. Its lead has two parts: a large number of covered returns and a high average income reported on each one. Population alone cannot explain why the totals differ.\n\nThe measure is **adjusted gross income, or AGI**, from the [IRS's tax-year 2023 county data](https://www.irs.gov/statistics/soi-tax-stats-county-data-2023). It includes taxable income from work, businesses and investments after specified adjustments, but before the standard or itemized deduction. It is neither the value of everything produced in a state nor all money received by every resident.\n\n## The top five, with the total opened up\n\nUSA Maps adds the published county amounts to construct state totals. Applying the same operation to the IRS's number of returns makes the total easier to understand:\n\n| Rank among 50 states | State | Summed AGI | Covered returns | AGI per return |\n|---|---|---: |---: |---: |\n| 1 | California | $2.051 trillion | 18,481,810 | $110,965 |\n| 2 | Texas | $1.270 trillion | 13,669,380 | $92,878 |\n| 3 | Florida | $1.102 trillion | 11,217,420 | $98,202 |\n| 4 | New York | $1.052 trillion | 9,789,240 | $107,451 |\n| 5 | Illinois | $605.6 billion | 6,100,310 | $99,278 |\n\nThe final column is an arithmetic mean, not the income of a typical person or household. A married couple filing jointly may appear on one return; some residents do not file. High incomes pull the average upward, and losses can pull it down.\n\nCalifornia has about 89% more covered returns than New York, but its AGI per return is only about 3% higher. Those two differences combine to produce a total nearly twice New York's. Against Texas, California has about 35% more returns and about 19% more income per return. The total is the product of both, not a choice between size and prosperity.\n\n## The income is concentrated inside California too\n\nFive counties account for **56.2%** of California's county-summed AGI:\n\n| California county | 2023 AGI in the county file |\n|---|---: |\n| Los Angeles | $472.3 billion |\n| Santa Clara | $201.7 billion |\n| Orange | $186.1 billion |\n| San Diego | $178.9 billion |\n| Alameda | $113.7 billion |\n| All other California counties combined | $898.0 billion |\n| California total | $2,050.8 billion |\n\nThe remainder makes the whole visible; displayed amounts are rounded. These are tax-return addresses assigned to counties, not the revenues or output of businesses headquartered there.\n\nSpecialized employment provides one concrete reason substantial income reaches this region. In May 2023, the [Bureau of Labor Statistics counted 96,590 software developers in the San Jose metropolitan area](https://www.bls.gov/regions/west/news-release/2024/occupationalemploymentandwages_sanjose_20240711.htm), with mean annual pay of **$199,800**. Computer and mathematical work made up 14.2% of local employment, against 3.4% nationally. That metro includes Santa Clara and San Benito counties; it is not identical to Santa Clara alone.\n\nThis is evidence of a high-paying cluster at a scale capable of affecting an income total. It does not imply that every California worker earns a developer's salary, or measure how much of the state's lead is caused by technology.\n\n## Paychecks are the largest source, but not the whole pot\n\nThe same county file records **$1.414 trillion in California wages and salaries**, about 69% of its AGI total. It also records $129.7 billion in net capital gains and $127.0 billion in partnership and S-corporation net income. These are separate published items, not a complete additive reconstruction of AGI.\n\nThat matters when comparing California with Florida or New York. The mix of wages, realized investment gains, retirement distributions and business income can change the amount reported per return. AGI includes realized capital gains that the Census money-income measure used in other USA Maps articles excludes.\n\nCalifornia's first place therefore describes the income reported on a particular set of federal returns. Its extensive filing base, major concentrations of well-paid work and substantial nonwage income all contribute to the scale. It does not establish the highest typical living standard, the best-paid ordinary worker or the largest state economy under every possible measure.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/california-tax-return-income.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/california-tax-return-income-640.webp","coverAlt":"State map highlighting California, Texas, Florida, New York and Illinois, ranked by county-summed 2023 adjusted gross income.","map":{"metric":"adjusted-gross-income-agi","place":"06","second":null,"caption":"California state observation · adjusted gross income summed from the IRS county-file state components · tax year 2023 · dollars. This is not state GDP."}},"garfield-work-from-home":{"published":true,"title":"Why 43.7% of Garfield County's workers work from home","seoTitle":"Why 43.7% of Garfield County's workers work from home","description":"Census cross-tabulations and USDA farm records explain Garfield County's 43.7% home-working estimate, including agricultural work and owner-operated businesses.","deck":"Additional Census tables connect most of the county's estimated home workers with agriculture-related industries or their own businesses. This is not evidence of a technology-worker boom.","body":"In Garfield County, Montana, working from home often means something quite different from logging into an office job. The Census Bureau estimates that **214 of 490 workers, or 43.7%, usually worked at home** in its 2020–2024 survey. Additional tables place most of those home workers in the agriculture-related industry group or in businesses they own.\n\nThe estimate is imprecise: an approximate 90% margin of error is **9.3 percentage points**. Garfield has the highest point estimate among the 3,141 eligible county rows in the retrieved map series, but the survey cannot establish an undisputed home-working capital.\n\nThere is, however, enough local evidence to explain the kind of work behind the number.\n\n## The commute question also catches work on a farm\n\nThe [American Community Survey asks people aged 16 or older who were employed and at work in the previous week](https://www.census.gov/topics/employment/commuting/guidance/home-based-workers.html) how they usually got to work. Someone reporting work at home belongs in the home-working category.\n\nThat includes people whose work is based at the home property. It does not require an employer in another city, an internet connection or a job in technology. It is also not a count of everyone who occasionally worked from home during the year; someone who usually commuted would ordinarily appear under that usual journey.\n\n## Agriculture is directly visible among home workers\n\nAn [additional Census table crossing industry with the journey to work](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B08126) estimates **147 of Garfield's 214 home workers** in the combined agriculture, forestry, fishing, hunting and mining group. That is about 69% of the home-working estimate. The grouping is broader than farmers alone, so it should not be relabeled as 147 ranchers.\n\nThe independent agricultural evidence makes ranch and farm businesses central to the local picture. The [USDA's 2022 agriculture census](https://data.nass.usda.gov/Publications/AgCensus/2022/Online_Resources/County_Profiles/Montana/cp30033.pdf) counted **238 farms**, occupying 2.33 million acres. Cattle and calves numbered nearly 70,000, and livestock and related products supplied 65% of agricultural sales. Ninety-one percent of farms were family farms.\n\nThose are businesses and land operations, not the same people or denominator as the commuting survey. They nevertheless show why a home-based workplace in Garfield can be a ranch rather than a spare room with a laptop.\n\n## Business owners make up nearly two-thirds of the estimate\n\nA separate [Census cross-tabulation by class of worker](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B08128) describes who the home workers work for:\n\n| Class of home worker | Estimated people |\n|---|---: |\n| Self-employed in an incorporated business | 65 |\n| Self-employed in an unincorporated business | 76 |\n| Employees of private companies | 56 |\n| Local-government and nonprofit employees | 10 |\n| Unpaid family workers | 7 |\n| **Total working from home** | **214** |\n\nThe two owner categories total 141, or about 66% of estimated home workers. An incorporated business owner is placed among wage-and-salary workers in some Census summaries; including that category avoids overlooking owners who put their business in a corporate form.\n\nThe industry table and this table describe overlapping aspects of the same workers. A ranch operator can be both agricultural and self-employed. Adding 147 and 141 would double-count people, and the published tables do not identify their exact intersection.\n\n## A small estimate still has a substantive explanation\n\nThe home-worker count has a published margin of error of 58 people, while the 490-worker denominator has a margin of 82. Those are margins around population estimates, not the number of survey respondents. The five-year period also pools responses from 2020 through 2024, including the pandemic years; it is not a 2024-only telework census.\n\nBut uncertainty does not erase the evidence of what people do. The survey directly connects home working with the local agricultural industry group and owner-operated enterprises, while USDA documents a landscape of large family farms. Garfield illustrates why 'work from home' can mean working where the business and household are based, long before it means remote office work.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/garfield-work-from-home.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/garfield-work-from-home-640.webp","coverAlt":"Montana county map locating Garfield County and labeling the estimated home-working share as 43.7%, with an approximate 9.3-point margin of error.","map":{"metric":"work-from-home-share","place":"30033","second":null,"caption":"Garfield County, Montana · workers who usually worked from home · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · percent."}},"oglala-lakota-international-income":{"published":true,"title":"Oglala Lakota's $11,771 income: an extraordinary gap inside America","seoTitle":"Oglala Lakota's $11,771 income: an extraordinary gap inside America","description":"Oglala Lakota's $11,771 annual income per person is far below the U.S. estimate. Household-income research tests the international angle, while local sources explain land, credit and jobs.","deck":"The county's annual money income per person is about one-quarter of the U.S. estimate. International household surveys provide context, but do not establish the proposed country equivalence.","body":"Could a county in the United States have income on the scale of Peru or an African country? **Oglala Lakota County, South Dakota, has an estimated annual money income of $11,771 per person**, including children—about $981 a month before personal income taxes. It is an extraordinary low point inside the United States. A claim that it is precisely equivalent to Peru, Mauritius or South Africa, however, is not established by the resident-income surveys.\n\nThe strongest compatible comparison is already stark. The Census Bureau's 2020–2024 American Community Survey puts the United States at **$44,673 per person**, about 3.8 times the county estimate. Oglala Lakota has the lowest point estimate among 3,144 county equivalents in the 50 states and D.C. Its margin of error is $1,892, compared with $122 nationally; the ranking orders estimates, not certain underlying incomes.\n\nThe local explanation involves a narrow employment base and documented obstacles to building businesses and using land, shaped by federal policies and the region's history. Community institutions are working directly on those obstacles.\n\n## A country comparison needs household income, not GDP\n\nNational economic output is not the same as money received by residents. More relevant national household surveys still use different definitions:\n\n| Place and period | Resident or household-income observation | What prevents a direct equivalence? |\n|---|---|---|\n|Oglala Lakota County,ACS2020–2024|$11,771 annually per resident, 2024 dollars|Pretax cash income divided by all residents, including children; excludes capital gains and noncash benefits.|\n|Mauritius, Household Budget Survey 2023|18,884 Mauritian rupees monthly per capita household disposable income; about $5,006 annually at the 2023 average exchange rate|After compulsory deductions and income tax; published household-per-capita averaging has not been harmonized to the ACS all-resident denominator. 2023 dollars are a different price year.|\n|Peru, ENAHO 2024|1,196 soles monthly real income per person, in 2024 Lima-price terms|A net-income aggregate including imputed housing and noncash items. Spatial price adjustment and tax treatment have not been harmonized to U.S. pretax money income; no nominal-dollar equivalent is asserted.|\n\n[Mauritius's report](https://statsmauritius.govmu.org/Documents/Census_and_Surveys/HBS/Analytical_Report_HBS2023_100225.pdf) and [Peru's report](https://cdn.www.gob.pe/uploads/document/file/8037677/6749463-evolucion-de-la-pobreza-monetaria-2015-2024.pdf?v=1748034232) measure household resources rather than production. That makes them relevant research, but not interchangeable statistics. The Mauritius currency conversion uses an annual market exchange rate, not purchasing-power-parity international dollars.\n\n[South Africa's 2022/23 income-and-expenditure survey](https://www.statssa.gov.za/publications/P0100/P01002022.pdf) is another useful check. Its headline R204,359 is average annual income **per household**, and includes imputed housing income and employer-related benefits. Comparing that household figure directly with an American per-person figure would manufacture a resemblance.\n\nThese surveys do not justify the headline 'an American county with the same income as an African country.' They do reinforce why a comparison must identify what people actually receive, how many people divide it and which costs and benefits the statistic includes. None supplies a price-adjusted standard-of-living equivalence here.\n\n## Schools, health care and government dominate the local employment picture\n\nOglala Lakota County is part of the Pine Ridge region, but **county, reservation and tribal membership are different populations**. The [Oglala Sioux Tribe](https://www.oglala.gov/) describes a reservation extending across a wider area and tribal members who do not all live there. The $11,771 measures county residents of all backgrounds, not all enrolled Oglala Lakota people.\n\nThe matched Census survey estimates **1,356 of 2,593 employed civilian county residents aged 16+** working in education, health care and social assistance—about 52%. Public administration accounts for another 365, about 14%. These are workers' industries, including people who may work outside the county; the numbers do not prove that all are tribal-government employees.\n\nNamed institutions make that service-based economy concrete. The [Pine Ridge Indian Health Service unit](https://www.ihs.gov/greatplains/healthcarefacilities/pineridge/?mobileFormat=true) operates a hospital and clinics. [Oglala Lakota College](https://www.olc.edu/about/history-of-oglala-lakota-college/) was chartered by the Tribal Council in 1971 to expand educational opportunity. Such institutions provide essential work and services, but their presence does not by itself create a broad private-sector labor market.\n\n## Land and credit help explain the difficulty of expanding that economy\n\nLand is an economic resource only when people can use it, coordinate its ownership and finance activity on it. The [Interior Department's account of allotment and fractionation](https://www.doi.gov/buybackprogram/fractionation) explains how land ownership divided among successive heirs, complicating development and infrastructure projects. This is not merely a distant national issue: [the department's Pine Ridge buy-back program](https://www.bia.gov/as-ia/opa/online-press-release/interior-announces-more-100-million-purchase-offers-nearly-16000) specifically addressed fractional ownership interests there.\n\nFederal land-taking also disrupted livelihoods directly. The [National Park Service records the 1942 taking of 341,725 acres of the Pine Ridge Reservation](https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/south-unit-badl.htm) for a military bombing range. Families were forced to move; compensation did not necessarily buy replacement land after prices rose, and some had to sell livestock when their grazing land was taken. That is a documented mechanism connecting loss of land with loss of an economic base, not a measured percentage of today's income gap.\n\nCredit is another concrete constraint. [Lakota Funds describes its 1986 founding](https://www.lakotafunds.org/our-story) as a response to limited capital, business assistance, networks and infrastructure. Its early lending supported entrepreneurs through small loans. The institution's history and [Federal Reserve reporting](https://www.minneapolisfed.org/article/2008/native-cdfis-work-toward-a-new-economic-reality-in-south-dakota) show local efforts to build a private economy rather than an absence of people trying to create one.\n\nMore recently, [Thunder Valley's 2024 report](https://thundervalley.org/annual-report-2024) describes construction training, housing development and a tribal land lease for buffalo restoration. These projects connect skills, land and local enterprise—the same kinds of resources whose absence or disruption can constrain earnings.\n\nOglala Lakota's low per-person income also includes a large population of children: about 35% of county residents are under 18. It should never be read as the salary paid to every working adult. The county's poverty and income profile is rooted in opportunities, institutions and access to economic resources. A country analogy cannot explain those mechanisms; the local record can.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/oglala-lakota-international-income.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/oglala-lakota-international-income-640.webp","coverAlt":"South Dakota county map locating Oglala Lakota County and labeling its annual per-person money-income estimate of $11,771, with the U.S. comparison shown separately.","map":{"metric":"per-capita-income","place":"46102","second":null,"caption":"Oglala Lakota County, South Dakota · average money income per person · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · 2024 dollars per person."}},"child-mortality-mississippi":{"published":true,"title":"Why Mississippi's child death rate is so high","seoTitle":"Why Mississippi's child death rate is so high","description":"Original CDC data clarify Mississippi's 2024 under 18 mortality and the map denominator. Infant health and age-specific injury evidence explain why an infant-only answer is incomplete.","deck":"The map combines 2024 deaths with an ACS2020–2024 child population. Original CDC records show that infant deaths account for more than half the deaths in its five leading states; older-child injuries matter too.","body":"Mississippi's high child death rate reflects **both deaths in infancy and risks later in childhood**. In 2024, the state recorded 568 deaths among residents younger than 18: 323 before the first birthday and 245 at ages 1–17. Its health reports identify premature-birth and infant-health problems alongside serious risks from road crashes and firearms.\n\nThe map's figure is **82.7 deaths per 100,000 children**, the highest among its 50 state observations. Its label needs care: the deaths occurred in 2024, while the population denominator comes from the 2020–2024 American Community Survey. It is not five years of deaths, an infant-only rate or an age-adjusted measure.\n\n## The original death records settle the period\n\nThe [CDC WONDER mortality database](https://wonder.cdc.gov/controller/saved/D158/D473F417) counts deaths from resident death certificates. A new query for calendar 2024, all causes and ages 0–17 reproduces the numerator behind every state row in the map.\n\n| Map rank among 50 states | State |2024 deaths, ages 0–17|ACS2020–2024 population under 18|Map deaths per 100,000|\n|---|---|---: |---: |---: |\n|1|Mississippi|568|686,805|82.7|\n|2|South Dakota|159|219,743|72.4|\n|3|Alabama|756|1,130,725|66.9|\n|4|Louisiana|722|1,082,850|66.7|\n|5|South Carolina|753|1,135,421|66.3|\n\nFour are Southern states. South Dakota is an important exception, so this is not an exclusively Southern top five.\n\nUsing CDC's own 2024 population denominator gives Mississippi a rate of **84.2**, rather than 82.7. It also changes the next positions: Louisiana and Alaska move ahead of Alabama in the official crude-rate ordering. A different population denominator can change a close rank even when the death counts are identical.\n\n## Infant deaths are a large part of the total, not the whole explanation\n\nA separate CDC query for children under 1 shows that infants account for more than half of 2024 under 18 deaths in all five map leaders:\n\n| State |Deaths before age 1|Deaths at ages 1–17|Infant share of under 18 deaths|\n|---|---: |---: |---: |\n|Mississippi|323|245|56.9%|\n|South Dakota|84|75|52.8%|\n|Alabama|410|346|54.2%|\n|Louisiana|407|315|56.4%|\n|South Carolina|441|312|58.6%|\n\nThese shares describe the age composition of deaths. They are **not percentages of the state-to-national mortality gap caused by infancy**. Infants are a small part of the child population, but their risk of death is much higher than that of most older children.\n\nMississippi's [2025 Infant Mortality Report](https://msdh.ms.gov/msdhsite/index.cfm/29,21453,299,pdf/Infant_Mortality_Report_2025.pdf) identifies congenital conditions, short gestation and low birthweight, and unintentional injuries among the leading infant causes. It also reports 73 sudden unexpected infant deaths in 2024. That umbrella includes sudden infant death syndrome, accidental suffocation in bed and unexplained deaths; it should not be treated as one proven mechanism in every case.\n\n## Care before birth and the sleep environment are documented intervention points\n\nMississippi's health department reports that more than 5,000 babies were born preterm in 2024 and 14.7% of births involved inadequate prenatal care. Its [Saving the Smallest campaign](https://msdh.ms.gov/page/23,30669,341.html) targets earlier care, maternal health and support for families. These observations identify real problems to address, without telling us what fraction of all child deaths a particular policy could prevent.\n\nThe infant report also discusses getting high-risk mothers and infants to facilities with appropriate neonatal care. For babies after discharge, safe sleep is a separate prevention target. Health risks before birth, medical capacity at delivery and the sleep environment are different pathways; combining them into one generic explanation would hide useful distinctions.\n\n[South Dakota's own review of provisional 2024 infant mortality](https://doh.sd.gov/health-data-reports/data-dashboards/infant-mortality/) likewise identifies congenital conditions, unsafe sleep and modifiable risks such as maternal smoking. It helps explain why a high child-mortality map value is not uniquely a Southern phenomenon. It does not establish that the two states have identical risks or health systems.\n\n## Older children face a different set of dangers\n\nMississippi's [Child Death Review report](https://msdh.ms.gov/msdhsite/index.cfm/31,21454,392,pdf/Child_Death_Report_2025.pdf) reports 66 motor-vehicle/transport deaths and 50 firearm-related deaths among children under 18 in 2024. The report's corresponding rates were 9.8 and 7.4 per 100,000, compared with 3.0 and 3.1 nationally.\n\nThe concentration shifts toward adolescence: 39 of the transport deaths and 40 of the firearm deaths were at ages 14–17. The all-cause death rate at ages 15–17 was 79.5 per 100,000, against 41.6 nationally. High infant mortality therefore cannot stand in for the experience of older children.\n\nThese injury categories answer different questions from homicide or suicide. Firearms can be involved in either intent, so firearm deaths must not be added to homicide and suicide counts as though all were separate groups. [CDC transportation-safety guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/transportation-safety/about/index.html) identifies restraints, safer driving and other proven measures as prevention tools; the state comparison alone cannot measure their exact contribution to this gap.\n\nThe state pattern is consequently more useful as a set of specific problems than as one regional label. Mississippi needs explanations and prevention spanning birth outcomes, infant care and safety through the teenage years. The age and cause records show why an all-children rate needs all of those parts.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/child-mortality-mississippi.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/child-mortality-mississippi-640.webp","coverAlt":"State child-mortality map highlighting Mississippi, South Dakota, Alabama, Louisiana and South Carolina, with the numerator year and ACS population period separately labeled.","map":{"metric":"deaths-under-18","place":"28","second":null,"caption":"Mississippi · deaths under age 18 per 100,000 children · 2024 CDC deaths divided by the U.S. Census Bureau ACS 2020–2024 child population. This source-map denominator differs from CDC age-specific rates."}},"rent-price-vs-burden":{"published":true,"title":"California has higher rent. Florida has more cost-burdened renters.","seoTitle":"California has higher rent. Florida has more cost-burdened renters.","description":"Florida renters have lower household incomes than California renters. Matched Census figures and rental-market research explain how a smaller rent can be harder to afford.","deck":"California's median gross rent is $2,036, Florida's $1,669. But Florida renters have lower household incomes, and a larger share spend at least 30% of income on rent and utilities.","body":"A smaller rent bill can leave a household more stretched. Florida's median gross rent was **$1,669 a month**, below California's **$2,036**, in the Census Bureau's 2020–2024 American Community Survey. Yet **60.1%** of Florida renters with a calculated rent-to-income ratio spent at least 30% of their income on gross rent, compared with **55.3%** in California.\n\nThe missing piece is income. The same survey puts [median annual income among renter households](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25119) at **$53,617 in Florida and $71,186 in California**. Florida has a lower housing bill, but also a much lower renter-income midpoint.\n\n## Compare the renters, not every household in the state\n\n| ACS 2020–2024 measure | California | Florida |\n|---|---: |---: |\n| Median monthly gross rent | $2,036 | $1,669 |\n| Median annual renter-household income | $71,186 | $53,617 |\n| Renters paying at least 30% of income, among computed ratios | 55.3% | 60.1% |\n\nThe [gross-rent measure](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25064) includes applicable tenant-paid utilities, not just the landlord's base charge. These are occupied-home survey figures, rather than current apartment advertisements. Money amounts are expressed in 2024 dollars; responses were collected across five years.\n\nLooking only at each state's income for all households would mix renters with homeowners. The renter-specific comparison is closer to the people facing the rent bills. It still does not join a particular median-income household to a particular median-rent home: the medians describe separate distributions.\n\n## What the difference looks like in a monthly budget\n\nConsider two explicitly hypothetical households, with annual incomes rounded near those renter medians. A Florida household receives $54,000 before tax, or $4,500 a month, and pays $1,669 in gross rent. Housing uses **37.1%** of its income, leaving $2,831 before taxes and all other expenses.\n\nA California household receives $72,000, or $6,000 a month, and pays $2,036. Housing uses **33.9%**, leaving $3,964 on the same before-tax basis. The second household pays $367 more for housing and still retains more money for everything else.\n\nThese examples explain the budget pressure. The observed 60.1% and 55.3% burden shares come from the [Census household-by-household ratios](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B25070), not from dividing one statewide median by another.\n\n## Florida added apartments while losing cheaper options\n\nThe University of Florida's [2025 Rental Market Study](https://shimberg.ufl.edu/publications/2025_rental_market_study.pdf) documents both sides of Florida's market. From 2019 to 2023, the state added nearly 195,000 renter households and more than 240,000 renter-occupied multifamily units. New construction therefore was occurring; saying that nothing was built would miss the evidence.\n\nBut the price mix changed. Between 2013 and 2023, Florida lost about **404,000 rental units costing $1,200 or less a month**, after adjusting 2013 rents to 2023 dollars. It added about 736,000 units above that price. A growing rental stock can thus offer fewer homes in the segment lower-income renters need.\n\nThe study also found a deficit of about 641,000 affordable and available units for households below 60% of their area's median income. 'Available' matters: an inexpensive home occupied by a higher-income household cannot simultaneously house a lower-income applicant. That research uses a different income framework from the map's 30% burden share, but it documents the shortage behind the affordability problem.\n\nCalifornia has its own severe housing constraint. The state's [Legislative Analyst's Office](https://www.lao.ca.gov/reports/2015/finance/housing-costs/housing-costs.aspx) documented a long-running shortage of housing near coastal jobs and restrictions that limited construction. Its 2015 analysis is historical context for expensive housing, not a numerical decomposition of the 2020–2024 Florida–California burden gap.\n\nNeither state offers one rent or one renter income. The practical comparison is the actual home available to a particular household, its utility costs and the income that household can reliably bring in. Florida's lower statewide price midpoint does not, by itself, make that budget easier.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/rent-price-vs-burden.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/rent-price-vs-burden-640.webp","coverAlt":"Two state maps compare occupied-home gross rent and renter cost burden in California and Florida, with separate dollar and percentage legends.","map":{"metric":"rent-as-of-income-rent-burden","place":"06","second":"12","caption":"California and Florida · renter households spending at least 30% of income on gross rent · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · percent of computed ratios."}},"state-poverty-high-income":{"published":true,"title":"New York alone clears both these poverty and high-income thresholds","seoTitle":"New York alone clears both these poverty and high-income thresholds","description":"All 50 states were tested against matched Census poverty and $200,000-household benchmarks. Only New York passes both; job and regional evidence explains the coexistence.","deck":"A screen of all 50 states finds only New York above the U.S. estimates for both official poverty and households earning $200,000 or more. Its economy contains sharply different kinds of work and opportunity.","body":"New York is the only state that clears both sides of this test: an official poverty rate above the national estimate and an above-national share of households receiving at least **$200,000 a year**. California, a plausible candidate, clears the high-income threshold but falls below the national poverty rate.\n\nThe rule is deliberately simple and fixed: compare all 50 states with the United States in the **same 2020–2024 American Community Survey**, and require both point estimates to be higher. It produces one qualifying state, rather than a top five manufactured by changing the thresholds.\n\nNew York's result makes economic sense. High-paid financial and professional work is concentrated alongside much lower-paid service work, and earnings opportunities vary across the state. A large economy can support substantial income at the top while leaving a sizeable population below the poverty threshold.\n\n## The result, and three useful near-contrasts\n\n| Geography | People below the official poverty threshold | Households with income of $200,000+ | Both above U.S.? |\n|---|---: |---: |---|\n| United States, the benchmark |12.45% ±0.08 |13.36% ±0.06 | Benchmark |\n| **New York** |**14.01% ±0.15** |**17.07% ±0.12** | **Yes** |\n| California |12.01% ±0.09 |20.50% ±0.11 | No: poverty below U.S. |\n| New Jersey |9.72% ±0.15 |21.64% ±0.16 | No: poverty below U.S. |\n| Louisiana |18.93% ±0.31 |7.80% ±0.19 | No: high-income share below U.S. |\n\nMargins shown are approximate 90% margins of error in percentage points, calculated from the published components. These comparisons describe survey estimates; they do not award statistical ranks to every state. The [complete 50-state screen](/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/state-poverty-high-income-screen.csv) records every result, including those not shown here.\n\nThe two columns count different populations. [Official poverty](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B17001) concerns people for whom poverty status can be determined. The [$200,000+ category](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B19001) concerns households. No claim is being made that the same households occupy both groups.\n\n## Finance is powerful without being everybody's job\n\nNew York's high-income industries are economically significant but employ a minority of its workforce. The [state comptroller's study of where New Yorkers work](https://www.osc.ny.gov/files/reports/pdf/where-nyers-work.pdf) found that financial activities paid average annual wages more than twice the statewide average in 2023, while accounting for 7.8% of jobs.\n\nThe same report describes a different labor market beside that one. About one in nine workers earned the state's minimum wage in 2022, the latest year in that analysis; the shares were 27.1% in leisure and hospitality and 22.4% in retail. Pay for a small, highly rewarded sector therefore does not translate automatically into comparable earnings for the much larger service economy supporting it.\n\nNor is a $200,000 household necessarily one employee earning $200,000. Two earners can combine salaries to reach the category. The measure includes other cash income too; it does not measure assets or declare every qualifying household equally wealthy.\n\n## A state total also combines very different regions\n\nThe comptroller found almost two-thirds of the state's financial-activities jobs and nearly three-quarters of its information-sector jobs in New York City in 2023. Other regions have different job mixes and pay levels. That concentration helps explain why statewide income statistics can contain both a large upper tail and substantial economic hardship.\n\nWithin the metropolitan region, the matched Census household medians range from **$146,202 in Nassau County to $48,676 in the Bronx**. Those figures are not poverty rates, but they make the separation in resident incomes tangible. Access to professional work, multiple earners and the housing through which people can enter a community all affect how income is distributed geographically.\n\nThe [state comptroller's poverty review](https://www.osc.ny.gov/reports/new-yorkers-need-look-poverty-trends-new-york-state-last-decade) also documents variation within New York and the greater exposure of low-wage workers to employment losses. A missed job, reduced hours or a period without earnings matters much more near the bottom of the distribution than the salary growth of a financial specialist near the top.\n\n## This is a distribution pattern, not an inequality league table\n\nThe screen does not measure the distance between every income group, and it is not a substitute for a Gini coefficient or another formal inequality measure. It finds a specific coexistence: New York has more official poverty than the national estimate and more households in a high-income category.\n\nHousing costs matter to living standards, but official poverty uses thresholds that do not rise with local rents. The Supplemental Poverty Measure treats housing costs, taxes and noncash assistance differently. Substituting that measure could answer a different question; it would not be an honest way to make California pass this one.\n\nThe useful finding is narrower and stronger than a forced list. Under the original matched-period rules, New York stands alone, and its concentrated high-paying work coexists with a broad, unevenly paid economy around it.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/state-poverty-high-income.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/state-poverty-high-income-640.webp","coverAlt":"Two state maps show official poverty among people and the share of households earning $200,000 or more; New York alone passes both national thresholds.","map":{"metric":"poverty","place":"36","second":null,"caption":"New York · people below the official poverty threshold · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · percent of people. The second article threshold concerns households, a separate measure."}},"population-largest-counties":{"published":true,"title":"Ten counties hold 12.3% of Americans. Why these places?","seoTitle":"Ten counties hold 12.3% of Americans. Why these places?","description":"See all ten largest county populations and the same-period U.S. denominator. Place-specific histories explain the jobs, transport and boundaries behind the concentration.","deck":"Los Angeles leads a list containing Chicago, Houston, Phoenix and Brooklyn. Jobs, transport and settlement help explain their size; county boundaries explain why the list is not a metro ranking.","body":"The ten largest U.S. county areas contain **41.31 million residents**, or **12.3% of the country's population**, in the Census Bureau's 2020–2024 American Community Survey. These are established urban centers and their surroundings, built through different combinations of industrial jobs, transport links, public investment and settlement.\n\nThe list also reflects how much of each urban region fits inside a county line. Los Angeles County contains a vast urban population. Kings County contains just Brooklyn. Comparing them ranks administrative areas, not whole metropolitan economies.\n\n| Rank | County | Estimated residents |\n|---|---|---: |\n|1| Los Angeles, California |9,808,667|\n|2| Cook, Illinois |5,182,090|\n|3| Harris, Texas |4,838,303|\n|4| Maricopa, Arizona |4,559,748|\n|5| San Diego, California |3,288,774|\n|6| Orange, California |3,165,820|\n|7| Miami-Dade, Florida |2,738,356|\n|8| Kings, New York |2,631,580|\n|9| Dallas, Texas |2,621,179|\n|10| Riverside, California |2,478,600|\n| |**Ten-county total**|**41,313,117**|\n| |**United States, same ACS product**|**334,922,499**|\n\nThese are [ACS period estimates](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B01003), based on responses and population controls across 2020–2024. The table does not show which places grew fastest recently, or how much migration each received last year.\n\n## Transport connected older centers to much larger markets\n\nChicago's position in Cook County developed with transport and trade. The [Chicago Public Library's planning chronology](https://www.chipublib.org/timeline-key-moments-in-chicago-planning/) traces the arrival of railways and the city's development into a national rail hub. The resulting industrial and commercial city accumulated residents long before the current survey period.\n\nTexas supplies two distinct versions of that process. [Harris County's historical record](https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/harris-county) links Houston's industrial expansion with the ship channel: access for oceangoing commerce helped attract industry and population. [Dallas County's history](https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallas-county) instead emphasizes the intersecting north–south and east–west railways of 1872 and 1873. An inland crossroads supported commerce and surrounding settlements without requiring a seaport.\n\nBrooklyn is the dense-city exception in the table. The [New York City landmarks agency](https://archaeology.cityofnewyork.us/collection/nyc-timeline/brooklyn-bridge-opens) describes the bridge opened in 1883 as a response to rising population and trade between Brooklyn and Manhattan. Later transport connected residential districts to employment across the city. Kings'2.63 million people are only one borough's contribution to that much larger region.\n\n## Public infrastructure and new industries expanded the West\n\nLos Angeles needed water as well as jobs. [The city's water department](https://www.ladwp.com/who-we-are/our-history/water-system-past-and-present) documents the 1913 aqueduct's role in making large-scale urban expansion possible. California's [State Capitol Museum](https://capitolmuseum.ca.gov/experiences/exhibits/called-to-action-californias-role-in-ww2/california-after-the-war/) traces the additional migration and industrial development accompanying wartime and postwar defense spending. Water infrastructure made settlement possible at a larger scale; employment gave people reasons to come and stay.\n\nMaricopa's Phoenix area is another case in which climate alone is an incomplete explanation. The [city's history](https://www.phoenix.gov/administration/departments/communications/history-facts/city-history.html) describes irrigation and wartime industrial development, while the [Bureau of Reclamation](https://www.usbr.gov/lc/phoenix/AZ100/1940/index.html) connects the area's transformation with military investment and the emergence of air conditioning. People did not simply discover a comfortable desert; infrastructure and technology changed what living and working there involved.\n\nThe other three California counties on the list have different employment and housing histories:\n\n| County | Documented part of the explanation |\n|---|---|\n|San Diego|[SANDAG's regional economic timeline](https://economicstimeline.sandag.org/) traces military investment alongside later scientific research, universities and technology. These are employment anchors, not merely a pleasant-climate story.|\n|Orange|The [county historical society](https://www.orangecountyhistory.org/wp/?page_id=38) documents military installations, postwar aerospace and light industry, suburban development and Disneyland's opening in 1955.|\n|Riverside|The [county economic-development strategy](https://riverside-county.digitalceds.com/economic-potential-challenges/) identifies developable land, housing relatively cheaper than coastal Southern California and a major logistics industry. Its residential economy is connected to the wider region, not isolated from it.|\n\nThose histories explain how large populations could accumulate. They do not assign percentages of today's population to a particular aqueduct, military contract or housing development.\n\n## Miami-Dade grew through a different gateway\n\n[Miami-Dade County's account](https://www.miamidade.gov/global/disclaimer/about-miami-dade-county.page?componentID=1553655066001&submit=true) marks the arrival of Henry Flagler's railroad in 1896 as the beginning of rapid modern development. It also describes the later arrival of Cuban refugees. Rail access, tourism and connections with Latin America helped shape a different metropolitan gateway from the manufacturing and rail centers farther north.\n\nMigration belongs in that documented history, but the population snapshot cannot establish a current inflow. A county can retain a large population built over many decades even during a year in which residents leave.\n\nThe 12.3% figure is consequently both a concentration finding and a geography lesson. The metropolitan systems behind these places spread beyond their county borders. The people counted inside ten particular borders account for about one in eight Americans; the economies connecting them reach much farther.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/population-largest-counties.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/population-largest-counties-640.webp","coverAlt":"U.S. county map locating Los Angeles, Cook, Harris, Maricopa, San Diego, Orange, Miami-Dade, Kings, Dallas and Riverside, labeled with population estimates.","map":{"metric":"population","place":"06037","second":null,"caption":"Los Angeles County, California · total population estimate · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · people."}},"youngest-oldest-counties":{"published":true,"title":"Why Madison County is young and Sumter County is old","seoTitle":"Why Madison County is young and Sumter County is old","description":"University populations and a retirement community explain Madison and Sumter's age contrast. The full county check also corrects Lexington and qualifies Loving's extreme estimate.","deck":"University populations help explain the low ages in Madison County and Lexington, Virginia. The Villages helps explain Sumter County. Tiny Loving County's 86.2 estimate tells a different statistical story.","body":"Madison County, Idaho, and Sumter County, Florida, have strikingly different age profiles: median ages of **21.4 and 68.5**, respectively, in the Census Bureau's 2020–2024 American Community Survey. A university brings young adults into the first county; a major retirement destination draws older adults into the second.\n\nNeither mechanism is mysterious once the places are identified. What needs more care is the apparent national record: Loving County, Texas, has an even higher point estimate of 86.2, but only 33 estimated residents and a 17.2-year margin of error. It cannot be treated as a securely established oldest county.\n\nA median is the age dividing the population into younger and older halves. It is not the arithmetic average of everyone's age, and a five-year survey estimate is not an exact count on one day.\n\n## The bottom of the age table includes colleges and children\n\nSorting the [complete county-equivalent estimates](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B01002) produces these five lowest point estimates:\n\n| Point-estimate order | County or county equivalent | Median age |90% margin of error |\n|---|---|---: |---: |\n|1|Madison County, Idaho|21.4|±0.2 years|\n|2|Lexington city, Virginia|22.6|±0.5|\n|3|Buffalo County, South Dakota|22.8|±2.9|\n|4|Radford city, Virginia|23.3|±0.4|\n|5|Kusilvak Census Area, Alaska|23.6|±0.3|\n\nThis is an ordering of estimates, not a set of statistically proven positions. Virginia's independent cities and Alaska's census areas count as county equivalents.\n\nFor Madison County, university scale is especially clear. [BYU–Idaho estimated that about 21,000 students were living in Rexburg in fall 2024](https://www.byui.edu/newsroom/enrollment-reports/byu-idaho-enrollment-grows-for-fall-2024). The county's ACS population estimate is 54,618. Those figures have different dates and methods, so they do not establish an exact student share. They do show an institution bringing a very large young-adult population into a relatively small county.\n\nLexington provides a similar institutional explanation within a much tighter boundary. [Washington and Lee reported 2,241 students in fall 2024](https://www.wlu.edu/accreditation-and-institutional-research/facts-and-stats/fall-enrollment-report-2024); [Virginia's higher-education authority recorded 1,527 at VMI](https://collegeoutcomes.schev.edu/rdPage.aspx?LinkHref=True&inpUNITID=234085&pagecalled=FactPack.Deep_KeyMetrics&rdReport=FactPack.Deep_KeyMetrics&rdRequestForwarding=Form). The independent city's survey population is 7,525. Enrollment is not the same as resident population, but two residential colleges make the concentration of young adults understandable.\n\nThis Lexington is **not Lexington County, South Carolina**. The South Carolina county's median age is 39.8, not 22.6. Confusing the names would turn a small college city's profile into a false claim about a much larger county.\n\nUniversities do not explain every young place. In Kusilvak, the same survey estimates that about 42% of residents are under 18. Its young population includes an unusually large share of children, a different profile from Lexington, where about 7% are under 18. A low median does not tell the same life-stage story everywhere.\n\n## Sumter is a retirement destination at county scale\n\n[The Census Bureau identifies The Villages as the major retirement community helping shape Sumter's age profile](https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2025/06/young-old-counties.html). The development extends across parts of three counties, so The Villages and Sumter County are not interchangeable geographies.\n\nThe difference in scale from Loving is substantial. Sumter's ACS population estimate is 143,408, and its median age of 68.5 has a margin of only 0.2 years. Retirement-oriented settlement brings older adults into a place in the same way that a residential university brings young adults into a college town, although the institutions, housing and reasons for moving differ.\n\n| Highest point estimates | Median age |90% margin of error |\n|---|---: |---: |\n|Loving County, Texas|86.2|±17.2 years|\n|Sumter County, Florida|68.5|±0.2|\n|Catron County, New Mexico|63.8|±2.1|\n|La Paz County, Arizona|60.2|±0.7|\n\nThe table makes the extraordinary Loving estimate visible without treating all rows as equally precise. Several counties tie at 60.0 below La Paz; the full ranking preserves those ties rather than forcing a unique fifth place.\n\n## Why 33 residents cannot establish a comfortable record\n\nLoving's estimated population is 33 with a margin of error of 30 people. That population estimate is not its survey sample size. Its age estimate's nominal 90% interval runs from 69.0 to 103.4 years; the extreme upper endpoint is a consequence of uncertainty, not evidence that the county's true median exceeds 100.\n\nEven Loving's population differs between Census products. A separate annual-estimates release puts it at 52 residents on July 1, 2025. That does not replace the ACS value or prove that 19 people moved in: the products and observation periods differ.\n\nThe useful contrast is therefore the well-documented one. BYU–Idaho and Lexington's colleges draw young adults; The Villages draws older adults. Their resident age profiles reflect those institutions and destinations. Loving's much noisier number belongs beside that explanation as a reminder that the largest value in a table need not be the strongest evidence of a national record.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/youngest-oldest-counties.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/youngest-oldest-counties-640.webp","coverAlt":"County map locating Madison in Idaho, Lexington city in Virginia, Sumter in Florida and Loving in Texas, with median ages and margins of error.","map":{"metric":"age","place":"16065","second":"12119","caption":"Madison County, Idaho and Sumter County, Florida · median age · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · years."}},"most-least-populous-states":{"published":true,"title":"America's five most and five least populous states: why the gap?","seoTitle":"America's five most and five least populous states: why the gap?","description":"Compare the same-release populations of the five largest and smallest states, then examine the documented jobs, transport, geography and settlement behind their differences.","deck":"California, Texas, Florida, New York and Pennsylvania lead the same Census survey. Wyoming, Vermont, Alaska, North Dakota and South Dakota have the fewest residents, for different historical and geographic reasons.","body":"California has about **67 times as many residents as Wyoming** in the Census Bureau's 2020–2024 American Community Survey. The gap is not explained simply by state size or warm weather. Large employment centers, earlier settlement and transport networks helped accumulate population in some states, while extensive rural and resource-based settlement characterized others.\n\nThe comparison below uses [one compatible Census release](https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT5Y2024.B01003) for all 50 states. 'Least populous' means the fewest residents. It does not mean the lowest number of people per square mile.\n\n## The five largest and the five smallest populations\n\n| Population rank among 50 states | State | Estimated residents |\n|---|---|---: |\n|1|California|39,287,377|\n|2|Texas|30,188,424|\n|3|Florida|22,416,077|\n|4|New York|19,852,366|\n|5|Pennsylvania|13,018,639|\n|46|South Dakota|907,428|\n|47|North Dakota|784,841|\n|48|Alaska|735,706|\n|49|Vermont|647,106|\n|50|Wyoming|582,397|\n\nThe five largest total 124.76 million residents. The five smallest total 3.66 million. Washington, D.C., has 681,294 in the same product; it sits outside the state ranking because it is not a state.\n\nThese are estimates associated with the 2020–2024 survey period, not a list of populations on July 1, 2026. A population total also records decades of births, deaths and moves; it cannot identify recent migration by itself.\n\n## The largest states did not all grow through the same route\n\n**California developed several large employment regions.** The Bay Area and Southern California give it more than one metropolitan anchor. The [State Capitol Museum's account of World War II and its aftermath](https://capitolmuseum.ca.gov/experiences/exhibits/called-to-action-californias-role-in-ww2/california-after-the-war/) describes how defense spending and technological and industrial expansion brought migration, jobs and extensive urban and suburban development. In Southern California, [imported water](https://www.ladwp.com/who-we-are/our-history/water-system-past-and-present) was an essential enabling investment. A favorable climate did not supply that infrastructure by itself.\n\n**Texas combined several urban economies across a large territory.** The [Texas Historical Commission](https://thc.texas.gov/learn/military-history/texas-world-war-ii) documents wartime manufacturing growth and the acceleration of urbanization. Houston's [ship channel and industry](https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/harris-county) and Dallas's [rail crossroads](https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/dallas-county) illustrate different routes into that urban economy. Texas's extensive land area allowed many settlements, but the employment and transport links explain why millions gathered in particular parts of it.\n\n**Florida's climate became more accessible through investment.** The [state's historical account](https://dos.fl.gov/florida-facts/florida-history/a-brief-history/world-war-ii-and-post-war-boom) links military training during World War II with accelerated highway and airport construction, followed by domestic and international migration. Tourism grew alongside construction, real estate, electronics and international banking. That documented combination is more useful than saying everyone moved south for sunshine.\n\n**New York built a commercial network early.** The [Erie Canal's history](https://www.canals.ny.gov/About/History) explains the connection between the Great Lakes, inland cities, the Hudson River and New York's port. It helped establish a corridor of settlement and trade that still organizes much of the state. New York City became a large employment and immigration center, while the canal connected upstate cities to that commercial system.\n\n**Pennsylvania retains a large inherited urban base.** The [U.S. Energy Information Administration's state profile](https://www.eia.gov/state/print.php?sid=PA) locates much of its population around Philadelphia and Pittsburgh and documents coal and other energy resources. The region's [coal and railway history](https://philadelphiaencyclopedia.org/essays/coal/) links industrial markets, transport and settlement. Pennsylvania's place in the top five does not mean it is currently growing faster than states below it; a large population built earlier remains large without winning a recent-growth contest.\n\n## Why the five smallest states are different from one another\n\n**Wyoming's settlement followed corridors through a dry interior.** [Wyoming historical research on the Union Pacific](https://www.wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/industry-politics-and-power-union-pacific-wyoming) traces the importance of railroad towns such as Cheyenne, Laramie, Rawlins and Rock Springs. Railways made settlement possible, but much of the surrounding landscape developed through ranching and resources rather than dense urban industry. The state's large map area therefore does not imply a large population.\n\n**Vermont has a much smaller territory and a persistent village-and-rural pattern.** The [University of Vermont's community assessment](https://www.uvm.edu/gund/vca/chapter-11-community-development) describes concentrated village and urban centers surrounded by dispersed rural households and working landscapes. It also documents out-migration during part of the 2010s. This is a different situation from Wyoming's large dry interior: small land area and a settlement system without a New York- or Los Angeles-scale center both matter to the comparison.\n\n**Alaska's enormous area contains unusually difficult distances and physical conditions.** [NOAA's regional account](https://www.noaa.gov/regional-collaboration-network/regions-alaska/alaska-regional-context) describes geographic isolation, extreme marine conditions and an economy anchored in oil and gas, fishing, mining and tourism. Those industries can create valuable output and strategic transport hubs without producing continuous metropolitan settlement across the land area. Alaska is the clearest warning against equating economic resources or square miles with numbers of residents.\n\n**North Dakota's major settlement expansion was agricultural and rail-based.** The [State Historical Society](https://www.history.nd.gov/ndhistory/settlement.html) documents homesteading and the railroad origins of Fargo and Bismarck. That history established a network of farming communities and regional service centers. It did not create a chain of port and manufacturing metropolises on the scale found in the largest states.\n\n**South Dakota has its own agricultural and resource history.** The [state's agricultural historic-context study](https://history.sd.gov/preservation/docs/SDAgriculturalContext2013.pdf) traces the role of railroads in locating towns and supporting farm settlement. The [University of Nebraska's historical encyclopedia](https://plainshumanities.unl.edu/encyclopedia/doc/egp.pg.077.html) records how drought and the Depression disrupted that agricultural economy. These are historical influences on settlement, not evidence that the state was losing residents during the ACS period.\n\nWarm Florida and cold New York both belong in the top five; compact Vermont and immense Alaska both belong in the bottom five. The ranking makes sense when the number of residents is connected to the particular jobs, routes and settlement histories that accumulated them, rather than reduced to one explanation for every state.","cover":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/most-least-populous-states.webp","coverSmall":"/ux/assets/articles/editorial-20261026/most-least-populous-states-640.webp","coverAlt":"U.S. state population map highlighting the five most populous and five least populous states, with Alaska shown in an inset and D.C. outside the state ranking.","map":{"metric":"population","place":"06","second":"56","caption":"California and Wyoming · total population estimate · U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 2020–2024 · people. State totals, not population density."}},"mississippi":{"published":false,"title":"How grocery access varies across Mississippi","description":"A distance-based USDA measure covers all 82 counties. The differences are large, but the map is not a measure of hunger.","meta":"USA Atlas editorial pilot · 8 October 2026 AI-assisted draft · owner review pending"},"electricity":{"published":false,"title":"California vs. Texas: the electricity price gap","description":"EIA’s 2024 residential averages show a 17.03-cent gap per kilowatt-hour. A price comparison is only the beginning of a bill comparison.","meta":"USA Atlas editorial pilot · 8 October 2026 AI-assisted draft · owner review pending"}};
